he value type); the empty-struct sentinel is `field.SubField == ""` instead. This is a per-package shape detail; the canonical fix is to read the type once at write time, not to introduce a pointer indirection or a package-internal nil sentinel that does not exist today. Worked instance: openapi.Parameter.Schema is a `Schema` value not `*Schema`, so the conformance test checks `param.Schema.Type != "string"` directly. The six surfaces are: (a) .github/workflows/ci.yml dedicated `OpenAPI schema conformance tests` step running `go test ./internal/controlplane/openapi/...`, (b) scripts/verify.sh `# 8. Required: OpenAPI schema conformance tests` header + literal command (BE-0381's prior steps 8/9/10/11 — govulncheck/staticcheck/golangci-lint/goreleaser — renumbered to 9/10/11/12 in the same edit), (c) CONTRIBUTING.md `8. \`go test ./internal/controlplane/openapi/...\`` entry under `## Required Checks Before Every Commit`, (d) SECURITY.md verification-gates table row + dedicated `## OpenAPI Schema Conformance Tests` section with a 13-entry closed substring set covering scope (internal/controlplane/openapi) / determinism (deterministic fixtures) / actionable-failures (operationId) / authorization (x-required-action) / opt-in-external (YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY) / envelope-pinning (yalla.output.v1, yalla.error.v1) / conformance-triple (TestOpenAPIConformance, TestOpenAPIEnvelopesReferenceStableSchemaVersions, TestOpenAPIExamplesAreRedacted) / redaction / CI-cadence (Every push and PR) / self-locating gate filename, (e) ralph/prd.json verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands entry inserted immediately after the httpapi entry (so the two adjacent commands are the two package-level subsets that follow the umbrella `./internal/controlplane/...` walker), (f) canonical reference conformance test file `internal/controlplane/openapi/openapi_conformance_test.go` + load-bearing conformance triple covering (i) end-to-end document conformance (3.1.0 identity, both response rows per operation, public empty security, authenticated ApiKeyAuth + x-required-action, required-string-in-path parameters), (ii) envelope schema_version enum pinning to `output.SuccessSchema` / `yerr.SchemaVersion`, (iii) example redaction via `output.Sentinel` with negative assertion against the credential-shaped `yalla_sk_<env>_<value>` pattern; the self-check `TestVerificationSuiteOpenAPIConformanceAnalyzerDetectsRegressions` drives every matcher with 11 synthetic known-good AND known-bad fixtures. Per-story knobs for siblings: (a) canonical subset command, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after the last required step; renumber the trailing optional steps in the same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 11-13 substring set covering every AC, (e) PRD requiredBackendCommands entry (insert near siblings in the array; keep the umbrella entry FIRST), (f) canonical reference test file path + closed conformance-triple func-name set + the `-run` filter prefix the canonical-funcs entries MUST satisfy. Load-bearing invariant: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, repository-integration-tests instance (BE-0380 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_repo_integration_static_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Repository Integration Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: repository integration tests (BE-0380)" subsection; SECOND worked instance of the BE-0379 verification-suite single-file template — applies to verification stories whose subject is a SUBSET of `go test ./...` (a specific package path, a specific `-run` filter) rather than the umbrella suite). EIGHT load-bearing rules: (1) SUBSET-OF-UMBRELLA REDUNDANCY IS DEFENCE-IN-DEPTH, NOT CHURN — when the canonical command (`go test ./internal/controlplane/store/...`) is a strict subset of an already-gated umbrella command (`go test ./...`), the new CI step / verify.sh step / CONTRIBUTING entry are NOT redundant: a future narrowing of the umbrella (e.g. `go test ./internal/...` minus store, a "skip slow tests" flag) would silently de-gate the subject while leaving the umbrella green; the dedicated step still fires AND surfaces the regression as a fast, named, targeted failure rather than buried inside the umbrella log. Cost: one extra ~30s parallel CI step. (2) VERIFY.SH RENUMBERING SAFETY — the verify.sh numeric prefixes 6-9 (optional steps after BE-0379's `# 4. Required: tests` and the bare `# 5. Required: race detector`) were not pinned by any existing test; inserting a new `# 6. Required: <subject>` between current 5 and current 6 and renumbering optionals 6→7, 7→8, 8→9, 9→10 was a safe edit. BE-0379's `# 4. Required: tests` pin is preserved verbatim. Future verification-suite subset stories adding a required step should follow the same pattern OR agree on a fixed prefix range like `# 6.X.` to avoid further renumbering as more stories land. (3) PRD HAS TWO COMMAND ARRAYS — verificationLoop.requiredLocalCommands (umbrella `go test ./...`, BE-0379) and verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands (per-package paths and per-`-run` filters, BE-0380 and later). Subset stories add to requiredBackendCommands; the JSON decode in the test MUST target that field. The two arrays must NEVER be confused — a regression where BE-0380's command lands in requiredLocalCommands instead of requiredBackendCommands would silently weaken the contract because the matcher decodes the wrong field. (4) PREFIX-TOLERANT FUNCTION MATCHER — the `func TestMigrations` matcher is intentionally prefix-tolerant (matches `func TestMigrationsForward` too) because the PRD's `-run TestMigrations` filter is itself a prefix match. Document the gap in a self-check subtest with a stricter fixture (one with NO `func TestMigrations*` declaration at all) so a future maintainer who wants exact-name pinning knows to switch to a regex anchored on `\(`. (5) ELEVEN-SUBSTRING SECURITY SECTION — the section MUST name every load-bearing AC fact: package boundary (`internal/controlplane/store`), determinism (`deterministic fixtures`), isolation (`isolated migrated`), bootstrap function (`TestMigrations`), actionable-failure tokens (BOTH `request_id` AND `resource_id` because HTTP-facing tests use the former and row-level tests use the latter — separating them keeps both contracts visible), opt-in escape hatch (`YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY`), deterministic fixture (`fake Dokploy`), redaction (`redacted`), CI cadence (`Every push and PR`), self-locating gate file name. Each substring is distinct and semantically anchored so Markdown line wrap cannot break the matcher. (6) SELF-CHECK PARITY WITH BE-0379 — each per-surface matcher gets BOTH a "fires on missing" AND "passes on complete" synthetic subtest. The CI workflow self-check uses a fixture containing the umbrella step but NOT the new dedicated step, asserting both halves of the matcher fail; the complete fixture adds the dedicated step and asserts both halves pass. (7) AGENTS.md SUBSECTION MIRRORS THE STATIC TEST — internal/release/AGENTS.md grows a per-story subsection mirroring BE-0379's structure: six-surface bullet list + closing paragraph saying "the matchers fail loudly on drift; the assertion IS the contract". The subsection heading is the story title verbatim so `grep "Verification suite:"` finds every instance. (8) EDIT-HOOK RETRY on .github/workflows/*.yml — the workflow security reminder hook may surface as an "error" on first attempt to a non-trivial workflow edit; the second identical Edit succeeds (the hook is informational, not blocking). Plan for one retry when editing workflow YAML. Forward-applicable verbatim to BE-0381 (HTTP handler contract tests, canonical `go test -run TestOpenAPI ./internal/controlplane/httpapi/...`), BE-0382 (OpenAPI schema conformance, `TestOpenAPI`), BE-0383 (policy matrix, `TestPolicyMatrix`), BE-0384 (quota concurrency, `TestQuotaConcurrency`), BE-0385 (job worker lease), BE-0386 (fake Dokploy contract, `TestFakeDokploy`), BE-0387 (redaction), BE-0388 (validator fuzz), BE-0389 (migrations from empty DB), BE-0390 (migration downgrade safety), BE-0391 (race detector — though this one is itself an umbrella story, may use BE-0379's template instead of BE-0380's), BE-0392..BE-0408. Per-story knobs: (a) canonical subset command, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (or fixed-range agreement), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 8-11 substring set, (e) PRD requiredBackendCommands entry, (f) bootstrap test function name (TestMigrations for BE-0380, TestOpenAPI for BE-0382, TestPolicyMatrix for BE-0383). Load-bearing invariant: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, HTTP-handler-contract-tests instance (BE-0381 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_handler_contract_tests_static_test.go + SECURITY.md "## HTTP Handler Contract Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: HTTP handler contract tests (BE-0381)" subsection; THIRD worked instance of the BE-0379/BE-0380 verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test mega-pattern: the static-test file pins SIX surfaces in one place — (1) .github/workflows/ci.yml dedicated `HTTP handler contract tests` step running `go test ./internal/controlplane/httpapi/...`, (2) scripts/verify.sh `# 7. Required: HTTP handler contract tests` header + literal command, (3) CONTRIBUTING.md `7. \`go test ./internal/controlplane/httpapi/...\`` entry under `## Required Checks Before Every Commit`, (4) SECURITY.md verification-gates table row + dedicated `## HTTP Handler Contract Tests` section with a closed substring set covering scope/determinism/actionable-failures/contract-triple/envelope-pinning (yalla.output.v1, yalla.error.v1)/redaction/CI-cadence, (5) ralph/prd.json verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands entry, (6) the canonical reference contract test file `internal/controlplane/httpapi/me_contract_test.go` AND its load-bearing contract triple (TestMeServerWritesResponseDataOnlyToResponseWriter, TestMeRequestLogRedactsBearerToken, TestMeErrorEnvelopeDoesNotLeakDependencyCause) so deleting the canonical example or renaming any of the three silently breaks the convention every other *_contract_test.go follows; the self-check `TestVerificationSuiteHandlerContractAnalyzerDetectsRegressions` drives every matcher with synthetic known-good AND known-bad fixtures so over-tightening and under-tightening are both caught at the package-internal API; REUSES the shared constants requiredCIWorkflowPath, requiredVerifyShPath, requiredContributingPath, requiredContributingSection, requiredSecurityPath, requiredPRDPath defined in verification_suite_unit_tests_static_test.go; when inserting a new numbered verify.sh step, ALSO renumber subsequent optional steps in the same edit because the numeric prefix is part of the contract shared between verify.sh and the static-test constants — skipping the renumber breaks the BE-0380 contract-prefix matcher).
- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, unit-tests-for-every-package instance (BE-0379 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_unit_tests_static_test.go + internal/credentials/keyring_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Unit Test Suite" section + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: unit tests for every package (BE-0379)" subsection; FIRST worked instance of the verification-suite single-file template called out in the BE-0354 dependency_scan pattern's closing line — applies to verification stories whose subject is the suite itself rather than a specific security control). NINE load-bearing rules: (1) SINGLE FILE pins ALL surfaces of the meta-contract so a future contributor changing the CI step name, the verify.sh step header, the CONTRIBUTING numeric prefix, the SECURITY row, or the PRD verification-loop array fails ONE test, not five. The single-file shape is the BE-0354 dependency_scan pattern carried forward to the verification-suite family. (2) CLOSED EXEMPT SET WITH RATIONALE — every exemption in allowedPackagesWithoutUnitTests MUST carry a rationale that points to where coverage actually lives, not just a "thin entrypoint" hand-wave. The rationale is what makes a future review decide whether to keep, extend, or remove the entry; without it the set drifts. Current exempt set: cmd/yalla (Cobra entrypoint, behaviour in internal/cli), cmd/yalla-api (process wiring, behaviour in internal/controlplane/*), cmd/yalla-worker (loop wiring, behaviour in internal/controlplane/worker), internal/controlplane/jobs (doc.go placeholder). (3) STALE-EXEMPTION GUARD (TestVerificationSuiteExemptionSetIsTight) — assert every exempt dir (a) exists and (b) still lacks tests. Otherwise a package that gains tests silently keeps its exemption and the discipline rots. (4) IGNORED-PREFIX WALKER — the package coverage walker MUST skip `.git/`, `node_modules/`, `dist/`, `outputs/`, `tmp/`, `bin/`, `vendor/` via prefix-with-trailing-slash check (the trailing slash is what prevents a hypothetical `output/` package from being accidentally skipped). (5) PURE FUNCTION SPLIT — findPackagesMissingTests takes (root, exempt, ignored) and is called once by the runtime test against the real repo AND multiple times by the self-check with synthetic trees built via t.TempDir + mkFile helper. The self-check is what proves the walker doesn't false-positive on test-only dirs and doesn't false-negative on ignored subtrees. (6) CASE-SENSITIVE SUBSTRING CONSTANTS — `requiredSecuritySectionSubstrings` are matched verbatim against the doc, so a substring like "every Go package" will NOT match a sentence-start "Every Go package". When the doc text naturally starts a sentence with the substring, use the capitalised form in the constant. The first run caught this: the test failed with `missing required substring "every Go package"`; the fix aligned the constant with the doc, not the doc with the constant. The self-check fixture (synthetic SECURITY.md inside TestVerificationSuiteUnitTestsAnalyzerDetectsRegressions) must be updated in the same edit — if the synthetic fixture starts to fail, the test diagnostic explicitly says "fix the fixture, not the test" to prevent the wrong direction of fix. (7) NON-MAIN, NON-DOC-ONLY PACKAGE WITHOUT TESTS IS NEVER EXEMPT-WORTHY — prefer adding 2-3 small tests (e.g. internal/credentials IsNotFound + IsUnsupported + Store-interface compile-time pin) over expanding the exemption set. The exemption set is reserved for thin main-glue and `doc.go`-only placeholders. Adding the keyring_test.go tests took ~70 lines and was strictly preferable to a `internal/credentials` exemption entry, because the package has real classification logic (errors.Is over upstream keyring sentinels) that a future upstream rename could silently break. (8) PRD AS A VERIFICATION SURFACE — the prd.json's `verificationLoop.requiredLocalCommands` array is itself part of the contract. An AI agent reads the PRD before picking up a story; pinning the suite command there guarantees the agent sees it without needing to discover CI workflows or shell scripts. The matcher uses encoding/json with a partial struct (`{VerificationLoop struct{RequiredLocalCommands []string `json:"requiredLocalCommands"`}}`) so a future PRD schema extension doesn't break it. (9) CI MATRIX AS PART OF THE CONTRACT — pinning ubuntu-latest + macos-latest + windows-latest is what prevents a silent "we only test on Linux now" regression. The matrix dimension is a separate substring assertion per host so the failure diagnostic names the missing host explicitly. Forward-applicable to BE-0380 (repository integration tests — replace findPackagesMissingTests with findStoreTestsMissingTenantIsolation), BE-0381 (HTTP handler contract tests — replace with findHandlersMissingContractTest), BE-0382 (OpenAPI conformance — replace with findEndpointsMissingOpenAPI), BE-0383 (policy matrix), BE-0384 (quota concurrency), BE-0385 (job worker leases), BE-0386 (fake Dokploy contract), BE-0387 (redaction), BE-0388 (validator fuzz), BE-0389 (migrations from empty DB), BE-0390 (migration downgrade), BE-0391 (race detector), and every future P=1 verification-suite story. Per-story knobs: (a) canonical suite command (e.g. `go test -run TestMigrations ./...`), (b) scope predicate (which packages/files the matcher walks), (c) doc heading constant, (d) substring-set capturing the ACs of that story, (e) per-story exemption set if any. The pattern is the next-strongest form of the BE-0354 (dependency_scan) single-file multi-surface pattern, applied to a meta-contract whose subject is the test suite itself rather than a specific security control. The two pattern families share: (i) single file, (ii) closed substring/constant set per surface, (iii) self-check with synthetic fixtures driving every matcher. They differ in: (i) BE-0379-family stories have a `findPackagesMissingTests`-style repo walker as their primary matcher (the security-control family has no such walker; their primary matcher is an AST scan over package source); (ii) BE-0379-family stories pin the PRD itself (security-control stories generally don't); (iii) BE-0379-family stories use a closed exempt set with per-entry rationale (security-control stories use closed constant lists like `forbiddenSecretEnvFragments` but no per-entry rationale).
- Security-verification cross-package secret-confinement pattern, Dokploy-token instance (BE-0361 pattern, internal/release/dokploy_token_isolation_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/config/dokploy_token_isolation_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Dokploy Token Isolation" section + internal/controlplane/dokploy/AGENTS.md "Token isolation (BE-0361)" subsection; SEVENTH worked instance of the BE-0344 two-test template and FIRST cross-package instance — confirms the template scales from "this single function MUST honour rule X" (BE-0346) and "this directory MUST NOT import surface Y" (BE-0357 admin endpoints, BE-0349 SSRF) to "this scarce in-process secret MUST flow from source-of-truth package A through redaction package B into wire-emission package C with NO leak surface in customer-facing package D". Six load-bearing rules: (1) TRAILING-SELECTOR-NAME matching — when the field name is unambiguously the secret in the codebase ("DokployToken" is unique to the Config secret field, no other struct has a field of the same name), match by `SelectorExpr.Sel.Name == "DokployToken"` regardless of the receiver. This catches `config.DokployToken`, `cfg.DokployToken`, `c.DokployToken`, and any future-receiver shape alike, in ONE matcher rule, where the package-qualified-only variant would miss the `c.DokployToken` form a method using a `*config.Config` receiver would naturally write. The receiver-name freedom is the load-bearing rule: any closed list of qualifiers (`config.`, `cfg.`, …) is brittle to local-naming choices in a future refactor. (2) RETURN-FALSE-FROM-INSPECT to suppress double-counting when a matcher fires on a parent node whose children would ALSO match — `config.EnvDokployToken` is both a SelectorExpr (X=`config`, Sel=`EnvDokployToken`) and contains an inner `EnvDokployToken` Ident that the bare-Ident matcher would also flag. Returning `false` from the `ast.Inspect` callback after emitting the SelectorExpr diagnostic stops descent and keeps the count at 1. The self-check fixture `handler that reads config.EnvDokployToken is rejected` pins this — without the `return false` it would fail with `got 2, want 1`. (3) COUNT-AND-PIN-LOCATION instead of count-only — for invariants where the secret MUST appear at exactly one structural location (here, `c.token` in `client.go`'s Authorization header), the matcher returns the full set of `file:line:col` triples across every production file in the package, the test asserts `len == 1` AND the single hit's path-prefix equals the canonical file. A count-only assertion would let a future refactor move the line to a different file silently; a path-only assertion would let a duplicate slip in. The two assertions in series are strictly stronger than either alone. The constructor's `token: token` binding (a `KeyValueExpr.Key`, not a `SelectorExpr`) is NOT counted by construction — `ast.Inspect` walks the Key as an `Ident`, not a `SelectorExpr`, so the matcher's `SelectorExpr` pivot skips it for free. The self-check fixture `constructor binding token: token is NOT counted` pins this distinction. (4) COMPOSITE-LITERAL-FIELD-VALUE inspection for redaction-seam invariants — `(*Config).Redacted()` returns a `RedactedConfig{...}` composite literal whose `DokployToken` field value MUST be a CallExpr to the local `redact(...)` closure. The matcher uses `findMethodOnReceiver(file, "Config", "Redacted")` (a small helper that walks `file.Decls` filtering `FuncDecl.Recv.List[0].Type` against both `*ast.Ident` and `*ast.StarExpr` receivers), then walks the method body looking for ANY composite literal containing a `DokployToken` KeyValueExpr, then asserts the value is a CallExpr with `Fun.Name == "redact"`. Three orthogonal regressions fire one diagnostic each: missing method, missing field in the literal, bare-selector value instead of a call. The same pattern applies to ANY future "method M MUST stamp field F through helper H before returning" structural rule. (5) USES-FIRST gate for defence-in-depth log surfaces — `(*Config).LogValue()` MUST call `c.Redacted()` first AND MUST NOT touch `c.DokployToken` directly. The matcher walks the method body, tracking `seenRedactedCall` (set when a `c.Redacted()` CallExpr is found) and emitting a diagnostic for EVERY `c.DokployToken` SelectorExpr seen. The two diagnostics are independent — a method that called `c.Redacted()` but ALSO read `c.DokployToken` directly would fire one; a method that omitted the call but had no direct read would fire the other; a method that did both would fire both. Forward-applicable to any "MUST consult helper H before reading secret S" pattern (variables LogValue, future credentials redactor, etc.). (6) MARKER-PATTERN runtime gate at the projection seams — three table-driven tests in `dokploy_token_isolation_test.go` install a fixed `BE0361DOKPLOYTOKENMARKERXYZ` prefix on the Config.DokployToken field and assert the marker never appears in (a) the `json.Marshal(c.Redacted())` byte stream, (b) the `slog.JSONHandler` output under `slog.Any("config", c)`, and (c) the `c.String()` debug projection — for EACH of three fixtures: short-token (canonical), long-token (longer than the redactor's sentinel, longer than any reasonable line-buffered slog chunk so a partial-flush leak would still surface), and collision-prefix (token prefix collides with the projection's field name `dokploy_token`, so a regression that truncated at the field-name boundary would still trip the marker check). The marker is the leak detector — asserting on token bytes alone would false-positive whenever the value happens to equal a substring of the stable projection text. (7) GOLANGCI-LINT S1025 gotcha — staticcheck (under golangci-lint v2) flags `fmt.Sprintf("%s", x)` as redundant when `x` has a `String()` method, suggesting `x.String()` instead. For BE-0361 the intent of the runtime test was to PROVE that `fmt.Sprintf("%s", cfg)` doesn't leak, but since `fmt.Sprintf` with `%s` against a Stringer DELEGATES to `.String()`, calling `.String()` directly exercises the same code path and satisfies the linter. The test comment must explicitly document the equivalence so a future reader understands why the seemingly indirect "%s" check is actually identical to the direct Stringer invocation. The same gotcha applies to any future Stringer-surface security test.
- Security-verification package-confinement pattern, backup-encryption instance (BE-0362 pattern, internal/release/backup_encryption_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/backup/encryption_isolation_test.go + SECURITY.md "## Backup Encryption" section + SECURITY.md Required Verification Gates table row + internal/controlplane/backup/AGENTS.md "Backup encryption boundary (BE-0362)" subsection; EIGHTH worked instance of the BE-0344 two-test template and FIRST whole-package-confinement instance — confirms the template scales from "this single function MUST honour rule X" (BE-0346), "this directory MUST NOT import surface Y" (BE-0357 admin endpoints, BE-0349 SSRF), and "this cross-package secret MUST flow through one seam" (BE-0361 Dokploy token) to "this leaf package MUST NOT carry an entire data plane". Eight load-bearing rules: (1) IMPORT-WHITELIST-AS-FORBIDDEN-LIST — the backup package's only legitimate I/O is os.ReadFile on the operator-supplied status path, so the gate enumerates the symmetric/asymmetric cipher set (crypto/aes, crypto/cipher, crypto/des, crypto/rc4, crypto/rsa, crypto/ecdsa, crypto/ed25519, crypto/tls), the archive/compression set (archive/zip, archive/tar, compress/gzip, compress/zlib, compress/flate, compress/bzip2), the database driver set (database/sql, github.com/jackc/pgx/v5, github.com/jackc/pgx/v5/pgxpool), and the process-boundary set (os/exec, net/http) as a closed forbidden map. The set is intentionally aggressive because the boundary contract is "no data plane", not "this specific library is bad"; a future contributor who needs one of these libraries must surface the design change in review, not slip it in. (2) INTERFACE-METHOD-COUNT-AND-NAMES — the read-only Reporter interface is pinned by name AND by its single method's name (Status). The matcher locates the InterfaceType named Reporter, rejects every field in iface.Methods.List whose len(Names)==0 (embedded interfaces would let any method through, e.g. embed io.Writer would silently expand the surface), and rejects every named method whose Name != "Status". A missing interface fires a separate "missing required interface" diagnostic — a future refactor that renamed Reporter to BackupSurface would silently disable the gate otherwise. (3) CALL-EXPR-SELECTOR write-seam scan — the package MUST NOT call package-qualified write/exec functions. The matcher walks every CallExpr, asserts call.Fun is a SelectorExpr whose X is an ast.Ident (package alias) and joins pkg.Name + "." + sel.Sel.Name to compare against forbiddenBackupWriteSeams (os.Create, os.CreateTemp, os.WriteFile, os.OpenFile, os.Mkdir, os.MkdirAll, os.Rename, os.Remove, os.RemoveAll, os.Symlink, os.Link, os.Truncate, os.Chmod, os.Chown, exec.Command, exec.CommandContext). os.ReadFile / os.Stat / os.Open are deliberately excluded — they are the legitimate read-only seams. (4) MARKER-PATTERN runtime evidence with FOUR encryption-shaped fixtures — a parameterised subtest installs the BE0362KMSMARKERXYZ prefix inside four payload shapes (kms-key-id with arn:aws:kms:, aes-wrap-blob with base64-shaped repeated bytes, restore-failed-message with multi-line bucket URL, pem-private-key with BEGIN/END markers) and asserts the marker never appears in (a) err.Error(), (b) every errors.Unwrap layer (TestFileReporterEncryptionMarkerSurvivesErrorWrapping walks the wrap chain — a regression that wrapped raw content inside a deeper %w would be caught), or (c) json.Marshal(Status) (defensive: the struct has no content field today, but a future Detail field would surface the operator's accidentally-seeded payload through every downstream projection). (5) FIXTURE-SHAPE diversity for redaction robustness — every fixture exercises a different class of regression a future "improvement" to the parse-error message might introduce; the marker is what makes the leak detector load-bearing across all four shapes, because asserting on the fixture's full content would false-positive whenever the content happens to share a substring with a stable error phrase (e.g. a fixture containing "is malformed" would trip the existing "backup: status file is malformed" message). (6) WRAP-ROBUST DOC SUBSTRINGS — when pinning Markdown content from a wrapped document (SECURITY.md and the runbook), choose substrings short enough to survive line wrapping. The first iteration used "Plaintext WAL must never" and "no read access" — both spanned line wraps in the source files ("Plaintext WAL must\n   never" and "**no** read access") and the test fired false positives. Fix: use "Plaintext WAL" and "read access" — same semantic, robust to Markdown wrap and to **bold** runs. The closed substring list still names every load-bearing fact (KMS-managed key for at-rest, read access for the no-access invariant, Plaintext WAL for the WAL ban, Auditability for restore audit events) without being brittle to formatting. (7) DUAL-DOC PIN — the gate pins TWO operator-facing documents: SECURITY.md (the public posture) AND docs/operations/backup-restore.md (the operator runbook). Each gets its own test function with its own heading constant and closed substring set. Pinning both prevents a future "tidy" pass from collapsing one without the other — the operator-facing runbook is part of the public contract because operators rely on it to discover the KMS rotation cadence, the no-read-access invariant, and the audit-event posture. (8) SELF-CHECK with KNOWN-GOOD AND KNOWN-BAD pairs for EVERY matcher — TestBackupEncryptionStaticAnalyzerDetectsRegressions feeds three pairs (imports, reporter-interface, write-seams) through every matcher. Each known-bad fixture must produce ≥N hits (N matches the synthetic regression count); each known-good fixture must produce zero false positives. The reporter-interface self-check has a THIRD synthetic — a renamed interface (Surface) — that proves the matcher fires a "missing required interface" diagnostic when Reporter is removed, not just when its method set widens. A self-check that only feeds known-bad through the matcher cannot catch over-tightening (a matcher that fires on legitimate code would still satisfy the bad-fixture assertions); a self-check that only feeds known-good cannot catch under-tightening (a matcher that fires on nothing would still satisfy the good-fixture assertions). Both pairs are load-bearing. Forward-applicable to ANY future security-verification story whose invariant is "this leaf package MUST NOT carry a data plane for cross-cutting concern X" — credential vaulting (the secrets package must not encrypt under a process-resident key), audit shipping (the audit package must not network out to a SIEM), rate-limit storage (the ratelimit package must not persist a token-bucket distributed store), backup restore orchestration (a future restore package's port shape), and KMS proxying (any future package that mediates KMS calls).

